<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The April rebound reflected markets pricing continued Hormuz-related risk before the May and June pullback set in. The final leg lower tracks the more hawkish dot plot delivered at Chair Warsh''s first FOMC meeting on June 16 and 17.</p>
<h2>Loss Aversion Makes the Decline Feel Larger Than It Is</h2>
<p>Kahneman and Tversky''s 1979 prospect theory work found that losses register roughly twice as intensely as equivalent gains of the same size. Applied to an anchor of $5,595, the current price represents a paper decline of about 25%, and loss aversion ensures that decline dominates an investor''s attention far more than the structural case underneath it. Bank of America''s June fund manager survey found 58% of respondents expecting stagflation, a backdrop that has historically supported gold rather than undermined it. That detail tends to get lost against the emotional weight of a quarter of a position''s value disappearing on paper.</p>
<p>The asymmetry matters because it pushes decision making toward the reference point instead of toward the actual opportunity in front of an investor today. Someone anchored to January is effectively answering the wrong question. The question that matters is what gold is worth from here, given the current setup. The question loss aversion keeps surfacing instead is how to get back to a number that was never a realistic baseline in the first place.</p>
<h2>The Disposition Effect: Holding for the Wrong Reason</h2>
<p>Hersh Shefrin and Meir Statman''s research on the disposition effect describes a related and well documented pattern: investors hold losing positions too long while waiting for a return to their purchase price, and sell winning positions too early to lock in the good feeling. A gold position built near $5,595 is close to a textbook setup for this. Selling now confirms the loss on paper. Holding defers that decision, even when holding is no longer being chosen on the position''s current merits, only on the discomfort of realizing what has already happened.</p>
<p>That distinction matters because gold''s structural buyers are not running the same calculation. The People''s Bank of China added roughly 9.95 tonnes in May alone, its 19th consecutive month of purchases, and global central banks bought an estimated 244 tonnes in the first quarter, according to the World Gold Council. None of those buyers are anchored to $5,595. They are operating on a reserve diversification horizon measured in years, building positions against a strategic case that has nothing to do with a single day''s print on a chart five months old. The contrast between that buyer and an investor frozen by a January high is the clearest evidence that the price itself is not what is driving the hesitation.</p>
<h2>Two Investors, the Same Price</h2>
<p>The same $4,167 print is being read in two different ways this week. One investor sees a quarter of a gold position''s value gone since January and feels the anchor pulling toward regret and a reluctance to act. Another sees a price still well above where the metal spent most of 2023 and 2024, with the longer running drivers, persistent central bank accumulation, geopolitical risk that has eased but not disappeared, and a Federal Reserve under new Chair Kevin Warsh that turned more hawkish rather than less at its first meeting, largely intact. The price has not changed between those two readings. What changed is the number each investor is comparing it to.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who added to gold near the January peak are anchored to that price and feel the 25% paper decline acutely, even if the position has been profitable on a multi-year view. Some are quietly wondering whether the inflation and safe haven case for gold was wrong all along. Others are simply uncomfortable watching a number they remember go down.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Gold is down about 25% from the record it hit in January, and I want to walk through why that number feels worse than it is. January''s $5,595 print was a one day spike during the worst of the Hormuz disruption, not a stable value, and it is not the right benchmark for judging where gold sits today. The longer term case for the position, central bank buying, ongoing geopolitical risk, and a Fed that just turned more hawkish under its new chair, has not changed. What has changed is the number we are comparing today''s price to. Let''s look at when you actually built this position and what it has done since then, not since a one day high.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who added to gold or gold miners specifically near the January peak, now anchored to a price the metal is unlikely to revisit soon.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with a long standing core gold allocation built over several years, where the January spike is a small part of the overall cost base.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients holding cash who have been waiting for a better entry point into gold or precious metals exposure.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull each client''s actual gold cost basis and purchase dates before the conversation, not the January high.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who added meaningfully in the four weeks around January 28 and flag them for a proactive call.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review whether the current allocation still matches the original purpose of the position: inflation hedge, portfolio diversifier, or tail risk insurance.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document the conversation, including the reasoning for holding or trimming, separate from the emotional reaction to the headline number.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A quick note on gold''s pullback<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our call about gold''s move this year. I wanted to put the numbers in writing. Gold peaked at $5,595 on January 29, a one day spike, and has since settled near $4,167, a decline of about 25% from that specific print. The metal is still well above where it traded through most of 2023 and 2024, and the broader drivers behind your position, central bank buying and geopolitical uncertainty, are largely intact.<br><br>
I would rather judge the position against when you actually built it than against a single high print. Let me know if you would like to revisit the sizing.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY gold investors:</strong> Self-directed investors who bought gold ETFs or bullion near the January peak and have spent five months anchored to a number that is unlikely to mean anything to their actual financial plan.</p>
<p><strong>Concentrated precious metals holders:</strong> Investors who built an outsized position during the early 2026 run and have no framework for deciding whether to hold, trim, or rebalance.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed gold has had a rough few months after January''s record high. I work with a number of clients who built positions during that run, and most of them were anchored to the wrong number when deciding what to do next. Do you have a few minutes to talk through how you are thinking about it?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor who bought gold near $5,595 has spent five months measuring every price against a one day spike, with no one to point out that the comparison itself is the problem. An advisor''s value here is not predicting where gold goes next. It is recognizing that the client is answering the wrong question, how to get back to even, instead of the right one, whether this position still matches the plan.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When did you actually build your current gold position, and at what price?</p>
<p>What was the original reason you added gold: inflation protection, diversification, or something else?</p>
<p>Has anything about that original reason changed, or has it just been the price that changed?</p>
<p>How much of your decision making on this position has been driven by the January high specifically?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Gold''s pullback and the number that is distorting it<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Gold is down about 25% from January''s $5,595 record, and I have noticed that number is doing a lot of the work in how investors are judging the metal right now, more than the actual price action since then deserves.<br><br>
If you built a position during the early 2026 run, I would welcome a short conversation about how to think about it without anchoring to a single day''s print. No obligation, just a second perspective.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
