<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Monthly averages are used for January through May to smooth daily noise; June is shown daily to isolate the move around the Fed''s June 17 decision. Source: X-Rates monthly averages, MTFX daily rates.</p>
<h2>RRSP and RRIF: The Treaty Exemption Still Does the Work</h2>
<p>US dividends paid into an RRSP or a RRIF are exempt from US non-resident withholding tax under the Canada-US Tax Treaty, provided the holding is registered correctly with the custodian. That exemption applies whether the underlying exposure is hedged or unhedged, and it makes the RRSP and RRIF the most tax-efficient home for US dividend-paying equities or ETFs in a Canadian household, independent of any view on the currency. The decision inside these accounts is a volatility and asset-mix question, not a withholding tax question.</p>
<h2>TFSA: No Treaty Protection, and No Credit to Recover It</h2>
<p>The treaty exemption does not extend to the TFSA. US-source dividends paid into a TFSA are subject to the standard 15% non-resident withholding tax, deducted by the custodian before the dividend is credited to the account. Because TFSA income is not reported on a Canadian tax return, there is no foreign tax credit mechanism available to recover that 15%, and the loss is permanent. A US dividend-paying ETF held inside a TFSA gives up a flat 15% of its dividend yield every year for the life of the holding, a cost that does not show up anywhere on a statement and rarely comes up unless an advisor raises it directly.</p>
<h2>Non-Registered Accounts: The Same 15%, Recoverable This Time</h2>
<p>A non-registered account faces the identical 15% US withholding tax on the same dividend, but the outcome is different because the income is reported on the investor''s Canadian tax return. The foreign tax credit allows that 15% to be claimed against Canadian tax otherwise owing on the same income, up to the limit of that Canadian tax liability, which converts a cost that is permanent inside a TFSA into one that is largely recoverable inside a non-registered account.</p>
<p>The practical conclusion follows directly from the account mechanics rather than from any forecast of where the Canadian dollar goes next. A household holding US dividend-paying equities across RRSP, TFSA, and non-registered accounts should generally fill the RRSP first, the non-registered account second, and the TFSA last, reserving the TFSA for Canadian holdings or non-dividend-paying growth positions where the withholding tax does not apply.</p>
<p>The Bank of Canada''s July 15 decision is the next point at which this calculus could shift again. A second consecutive hold against a Fed that has already moved would extend the policy gap behind this year''s currency move. A reason to revisit the hedging decision, on either side, would be a change in either bank''s path, not a change in the account rules that determine where the tax drag actually falls.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are not usually tracking the Canadian dollar day to day, so the reaction here is more often surprise than anxiety, a sense that returns on their US holdings have quietly moved for reasons unrelated to the stocks themselves. Clients who chose a TFSA for US dividend-paying ETFs on convenience rather than analysis may feel mildly frustrated once the permanent withholding cost is explained.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Canadian dollar has weakened to its softest level in over a year, and that has actually added to the return on the unhedged portion of your US holdings recently. Separately, while we are looking at your account structure, I want to flag something worth checking: US dividend-paying positions are most tax-efficient inside an RRSP, where they are exempt from US withholding tax. Inside a TFSA, that exemption does not apply, and the 15% withholding cannot be recovered. None of this requires action driven by where the currency goes next, but it is worth reviewing which account is holding which type of US exposure.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients holding US dividend-paying stocks or ETFs inside a TFSA, where the 15% withholding tax is a permanent, unrecoverable drag.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients holding a mix of hedged and unhedged US equity funds across registered and non-registered accounts without a clear rationale for the split.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with unhedged US equity exposure concentrated in an RRSP or RRIF, who are receiving the currency tailwind without any withholding tax drag.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull each household''s account-by-account breakdown of US dividend-paying holdings before the next review.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag any US dividend-paying ETF or stock currently held inside a TFSA for discussion.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm whether existing US equity exposure is hedged or unhedged at the fund level, not just at the asset-class level.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar the July 15 Bank of Canada decision as the next checkpoint for revisiting the hedging conversation.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A quick note on your US holdings and account placement<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation about the Canadian dollar''s recent move. As discussed, the dollar has weakened to about $1.4167 per US dollar, its softest level in over a year, which has added a modest tailwind to any unhedged US equity exposure you hold.<br><br>
Separately, I want to flag the account placement question we touched on. US dividend-paying positions are more tax-efficient inside an RRSP or RRIF, where they are exempt from US withholding tax, than inside a TFSA, where a 15% withholding tax applies and cannot be recovered. I would like to review where your US dividend payers currently sit and whether moving any of them makes sense.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with US dividend ETFs in a TFSA:</strong> Self-directed investors who built a US dividend income strategy inside a TFSA without realizing the 15% withholding tax cannot be recovered there.</p>
<p><strong>Cross-border or dual-currency households:</strong> Investors holding meaningful US-dollar exposure across multiple account types with no coordinated view of where that exposure sits.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed the Canadian dollar just hit its weakest level in over a year, and it made me think about a detail that catches a lot of self-directed investors off guard: where you hold your US dividend stocks can cost you 15% a year in withholding tax that you can never get back. Do you know which of your accounts are holding US dividend payers right now?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor managing their own RRSP, TFSA, and non-registered accounts is unlikely to be tracking which account holds which US dividend payer, let alone the treaty mechanics that make a TFSA the worst possible home for that exposure. An advisor''s value here is structural rather than predictive: catching a 15% permanent drag that has nothing to do with stock selection and everything to do with account placement.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you currently hold any US dividend-paying stocks or ETFs inside a TFSA?</p>
<p>Has anyone reviewed which of your accounts are most tax-efficient for US-dollar income specifically?</p>
<p>Are your US equity holdings currency-hedged, unhedged, or a mix you have not fully mapped out?</p>
<p>How are you currently deciding which holdings go into which account type?</p>
</div>
