</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">April and May figures are EIA monthly averages rather than single-day prints; the two June points are daily spot prices. Source: US Energy Information Administration Short-Term Energy Outlook.</p>
<h2>Tail Risk: The Lebanon Condition Iran Keeps Attaching</h2>
<p>The market is currently pricing continued de-escalation as the dominant scenario, evidenced by oil extending its decline through this week despite a weekend wrinkle. Iran briefly announced over the weekend that it had again halted Strait traffic, explicitly tying further negotiation progress to an end to the conflict in Lebanon, even as tanker tracking data showed millions of barrels continuing to move through the chokepoint regardless. Iran''s nuclear program is a second, separate flashpoint: Vice President JD Vance said Tehran had agreed to admit nuclear inspectors, a claim Iranian officials have denied outright. Either thread, Lebanon or the inspector question, looks like a more plausible trigger for the framework unraveling than a reversal of the oil license itself.</p>
<h2>What Determines Whether the Window Holds</h2>
<p>The clearest real-time signal here is not the diplomatic language but the tanker data. Continued Gulf producer ramp-up from Kuwait and the UAE, alongside continued Iranian shipments at a discount to Chinese buyers, suggests market participants are pricing the framework as durable enough to act on rather than waiting for a final signature. The most relevant marker for Canadian portfolios over the rest of the 60-day window is not a specific date so much as a pattern: any verified interruption to Hormuz traffic that holds for more than a news cycle would be the signal that the de-escalation case is breaking down, and it would matter more to Canadian energy valuations than anything said in Geneva.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with concentrated Canadian energy exposure built up over the past four months may feel uneasy watching oil prices fall after being told for months that the Hormuz disruption justified the overweight. Some may worry the trade is now broken, or that they have missed the moment to take profits.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Oil prices have come down to a three-month low after the US and Iran signed a framework in Geneva and Washington gave Iran a 60-day license to sell oil internationally. The Canadian energy re-rating we discussed earlier this year was always tied to that shipping lane being closed, not to a change in Canadian production or margins, so it makes sense that the move is unwinding now that barrels are flowing again. This does not necessarily mean the position is wrong, but it is a good time to revisit how large that exposure has become relative to your overall plan, especially since there is still a real risk the framework could unravel over Lebanon or the nuclear inspection question.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated, single-name Canadian energy positions added specifically since the conflict began in late February.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with a modest, diversified energy sector weighting as part of a broader Canadian equity allocation.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who stayed underweight energy through the rally and now have room to rebalance into other sectors at improved relative valuations.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who added to Canadian energy names specifically since February 28 and flag them for review.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check current energy sector weight against each client''s long-term target allocation, not against recent performance alone.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the Lebanon and nuclear-inspector flashpoints as the two specific risks that could reverse the current de-escalation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Avoid framing this as a binary sell or hold decision in client conversations; frame it as a position-sizing review.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A quick note on oil prices and your energy exposure<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation about oil prices and your Canadian energy holdings. Crude has fallen to a three-month low after the US and Iran signed a framework in Geneva and Washington issued a 60-day license for Iran to sell oil internationally.<br><br>
As discussed, the run-up in Canadian energy names this year was tied closely to the Strait of Hormuz disruption rather than to a change in underlying fundamentals, so this pullback is consistent with that story unwinding, not contradicting it. There is still a real chance the framework could break down over Lebanon or the nuclear inspection question, so I am not suggesting urgency, just a review of position size.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY energy stock investors:</strong> Self-directed investors who bought Canadian energy names during the war premium without a framework for deciding when to trim.</p>
<p><strong>Concentrated single-stock holders:</strong> Investors holding an outsized position in one or two Canadian energy producers acquired specifically since the conflict began.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed oil prices just hit a three-month low after the framework signed in Geneva, and it made me think of investors who built up Canadian energy positions earlier this year on the Hormuz story. Do you have a plan for what happens to that position now that the shipping lane is reopening?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor who added Canadian energy exposure during the Hormuz disruption is now facing the unwind of that trade without necessarily having a framework for position sizing or rebalancing. An advisor''s value here is structural: separating a sector bet that was really a geopolitical bet from the long-term plan, and building a position-sizing approach that survives the next headline either way.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Did you add to Canadian energy positions since the Middle East conflict began in late February?</p>
<p>Do you have a plan for what happens to that position if oil prices continue falling toward pre-conflict levels?</p>
<p>How concentrated is your current energy sector exposure relative to the rest of your portfolio?</p>
<p>Who is helping you separate the geopolitical risk in this trade from the underlying business fundamentals?</p>
</div>
